The rear camera's focus motor: it was missing an enable, not a driver

WHAT WAS BELIEVED
-----------------
That the actuator did not exist or was not reachable. Scanning the sensor's CCI
bus with the camera powered, only the sensor (0x1a) and the EEPROM (0x50)
answered, and that was taken as conclusive. The last 2.8 V rail that was still
off was even turned on (patch 0097) in case it was a lack of power, and it did
not show up either.

WHAT IT ACTUALLY WAS
--------------------
An unowned pin. In the SoC itself:

    line 66: "64M_AF_EN"  input

"64M" is the 64 MP camera, that is, the main rear one, and "AF_EN" is autofocus
enable. `gpioinfo` reported it as an input, with no consumer: nobody was driving
it.

Driving it high, with the rear camera streaming:

    before:  00:  -- -- -- -- -- -- -- --
    after:   00:  -- -- -- -- 0c -- -- --

0x0c is the classic address of Dongwoon VCMs. It shows up in BOTH scan modes
-read and quick write- and accepts position commands with the two-byte protocol:
0, 256, 512, 768 and 1023 were tried, all accepted.

An unenabled chip does not answer however well you ask it, and the earlier scan
was correct: it was the premise that failed, not the method.

IT IS THE SAME FAULT THE WIDE ANGLE HAD
---------------------------------------
The wide angle was missing CAM_SW2_OE_2, another enable that nobody declared, and
it turned up the same way: by reading the NAMES of the GPIO lines. The technique
had already worked on this phone and had not been applied here.

WHY dw9807-vcm AND NOT dw9714
-----------------------------
The first attempt used dw9714, which sends TWO RAW BYTES with the position. The
chip acknowledged every write... and the lens did not move. The reason is that
those two bytes land on registers 0x00 and 0x01, which are identification and
control: the chip accepts them and does nothing with them.

This chip speaks BY REGISTERS -address plus data- with the ten-bit position split
between 0x03 (two high bits) and 0x04 (eight low ones). It was checked by
reading: the registers return what is written to them, 0x00/0x00 for position 0
and 0x03/0xff for 1023.

And it was checked that it MOVES, which is what matters:

    dw9714 protocol (two raw bytes):
      sharpness varies by x1.01 at random between the two extremes

    protocol by registers:
      position    sharpness  contrast
             0     85443.2      117
           340     78526.2      119
           680     67490.3       87
          1023     66997.3       82

Monotonic, and the contrast falls along with it. That is progressive defocus;
noise does not do that.

Mainline's dw9807-vcm writes exactly that format: register 0x03 followed by the
two position bytes. That is why that driver is used and there is no need to write
one.

With this, V4L2_CID_FOCUS_ABSOLUTE appears, from 0 to 1023: MANUAL focus. The
automatic one goes above, in whoever has the frames.

AND lens-focus IS NEEDED IN THE SENSOR NODE
-------------------------------------------
With the actuator declared on its own, the driver binds to the chip -you can see
it in /sys/bus/i2c/devices/12-000c/driver, and the regulator shows up with
12-000c-vcc as consumer- but NO subdevice and NO focus control appears. Nobody
puts it into the graph.

The missing piece is the lens-focus property in the sensor node:
v4l2_async_register_subdev_sensor(), which is what the IMX682 driver uses, reads
it and creates the ancillary link. That is where the node comes from and, with
it, V4L2_CID_FOCUS_ABSOLUTE.

THE ENABLE HAS TO STAY ON
-------------------------
The driver turns off the actuator's regulator when suspending, and to wake up it
needs to talk to the chip over i2c... but the CCI bus only lives while the SENSOR
is streaming. Result: it suspends, is left without power and cannot come back.
Measured: enable count 0 and runtime_status = error, with the subdevice refusing
to open.

With regulator-always-on the chip keeps its power and the driver depends only on
the bus, which is a dependency that can be met: moving focus while capturing.

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm7150-xiaomi-surya.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm7150-xiaomi-surya.dtsi
@@ -26,6 +26,49 @@
 		vin-supply = <&vreg_bob>;
 	};
 
+	/*
+	 * EL HABILITADOR DEL MOTOR DE ENFOQUE.
+	 *
+	 * La linea se llama "64M_AF_EN" en el propio SoC -64M es la camara de
+	 * 64 MP, o sea la principal trasera- y venia SIN DUENNO: `gpioinfo` la
+	 * daba como "input", sin conductor.
+	 *
+	 * Ese pin es la razon de que el actuador pareciera no existir. Con el
+	 * bajo, barrer el bus entero de su sensor no encuentra nada; poniendolo
+	 * alto, aparece 0x0c -la direccion clasica de los VCM Dongwoon- en los
+	 * DOS modos de barrido, y acepta ordenes de posicion.
+	 *
+	 * Es exactamente el mismo tipo de fallo que tenia el gran angular con
+	 * CAM_SW2_OE_2: un habilitador que nadie declaraba.
+	 */
+	vreg_cam_af_en: regulator-vreg-cam-af-en {
+		compatible = "regulator-fixed";
+		regulator-name = "cam_af_en";
+		regulator-min-microvolt = <2800000>;
+		regulator-max-microvolt = <2800000>;
+
+		gpio = <&tlmm 66 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		enable-active-high;
+		startup-delay-us = <100>;
+
+		/*
+		 * SIEMPRE ENCENDIDO, y no es pereza.
+		 *
+		 * El driver del actuador apaga este regulador al suspenderse, y
+		 * para volver a encenderse necesita hablar con el chip por i2c.
+		 * Pero el bus CCI solo vive mientras el SENSOR transmite, asi que
+		 * en cuanto se suspendia se quedaba fuera: el regulador a cero, el
+		 * estado de energia en "error" y el subdispositivo sin abrirse.
+		 *
+		 * Medido: enable count 0 y /sys/.../power/runtime_status = error.
+		 *
+		 * Dejando el habilitador fijo, el chip conserva la corriente y el
+		 * driver solo depende del bus, que es una dependencia que si se
+		 * puede cumplir -moviendo el foco mientras se captura-.
+		 */
+		regulator-always-on;
+	};
+
 	vreg_uw_ldo_ois_drv: regulator-vreg-uw-ldo-ois-drv {
 		compatible = "regulator-fixed";
 		regulator-name = "uw_ldo_ois_drv";
@@ -1134,11 +1177,52 @@
 	 * REACTIVADA para bisecar: hace falta un segundo sensor en otro CSIPHY
 	 * para separar "falla nuestra camara" de "falla CAMSS en este SoC".
 	 */
+	/*
+	 * EL MOTOR DE ENFOQUE de la principal.
+	 *
+	 * El fichero de fabrica lo llama "ofilm_dw9800", y este chip habla POR
+	 * REGISTROS: direccion mas dato, con la posicion de diez bits repartida
+	 * entre 0x03 (los dos bits altos) y 0x04 (los ocho bajos).
+	 *
+	 * NO es el protocolo del dw9714, que manda dos bytes crudos. Se probo
+	 * con ese driver primero y el resultado enganna: el chip CONFIRMA cada
+	 * escritura -porque cae sobre los registros 0x00 y 0x01, que son
+	 * identificacion y control- y la lente no se mueve. Medido: con dw9714
+	 * la nitidez variaba x1,01 al azar entre los dos extremos; escribiendo
+	 * por registros baja de forma monotona, 85443 -> 66997, y el contraste
+	 * cae con ella de 117 a 82. Eso es desenfoque progresivo.
+	 *
+	 * dw9807-vcm de mainline escribe exactamente eso: el registro 0x03
+	 * seguido de los dos bytes de posicion. Por eso se usa ese.
+	 *
+	 * Con esto sale V4L2_CID_FOCUS_ABSOLUTE de 0 a 1023, o sea enfoque
+	 * manual. El automatico va por encima, en quien tenga los fotogramas.
+	 */
+	cam_focus: focus@c {
+		compatible = "dongwoon,dw9807-vcm";
+		reg = <0x0c>;
+		vcc-supply = <&vreg_cam_af_en>;
+	};
+
 	camera@1a {
 		compatible = "sony,imx682";
 		reg = <0x1a>;
 
 		/*
+		 * EL MOTOR ES SUYO, Y HAY QUE DECIRLO AQUI.
+		 *
+		 * Sin esta referencia el driver del actuador engancha con el chip
+		 * -se ve en /sys/bus/i2c/devices/12-000c/driver- pero no aparece
+		 * ningun subdispositivo ni ningun control de enfoque: nadie lo
+		 * mete en el grafo.
+		 *
+		 * v4l2_async_register_subdev_sensor(), que es lo que usa este
+		 * driver, lee lens-focus y crea el enlace auxiliar. De ahi sale
+		 * el nodo y, con el, V4L2_CID_FOCUS_ABSOLUTE.
+		 */
+		lens-focus = <&cam_focus>;
+
+		/*
 		 * MEDIDO, no deducido: se puso el movil de pie apuntando a la
 		 * habitacion y se miro la captura en crudo. La imagen del sensor
 		 * hay que girarla 90 grados EN SENTIDO HORARIO para que quede
